This is an unofficial fork of whipper (https://github.com/whipper-team/whipper),
a GPLv3-licensed CD-DA ripper. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by
the whipper-team/whipper project or its contributors.

Per GPLv3 section 5(c), this NOTICE lists the substantive modifications
made to the original work. See the git commit history for full detail.

Base: whipper's `develop` branch, at commit 71251a0 (2026-09-02).

Modifications:

- Cover art fetching rewritten as a configurable provider chain (Cover
  Art Archive at the release and release-group level, iTunes, and
  optionally fanart.tv/Discogs/Last.fm), replacing the original's single
  hardcoded Cover Art Archive call. Reliability fixes: cover art is
  written atomically into the destination directory instead of the
  system temp directory, provider failures no longer abort the whole
  fetch, and embedded pictures are verified by reopening the file after
  writing (catching a historical bug class where embedding silently did
  nothing).

- Metadata lookup extended with a fallback chain: MusicBrainz, then
  FreeDB (previously looked up but never actually used), then Discogs
  (via an explicit --search hint, since Discogs has no disc-ID lookup),
  then the disc's own embedded CD-Text as a last resort.

- Encoding rewritten to use ffmpeg instead of the standalone `flac`
  binary, supporting FLAC (default, unchanged), WAV, ALAC, MP3, Opus,
  Vorbis, and AAC. Tagging and cover art embedding generalized to work
  across all of those containers (Vorbis comments, ID3, or MP4 atoms, as
  appropriate) instead of FLAC only.

- AccurateRip verification decoupled from the output encoding: checksums
  are computed once, during ripping, from the pre-encode WAV, rather
  than by re-decoding whichever final output file happened to exist --
  necessary because AccurateRip verification previously assumed a
  FLAC/WAV output, which no longer holds once other formats are
  supported.

- Various smaller fixes made along the way: a Python version floor that
  didn't match what the code already required, several pre-existing
  flake8 violations, a test that wrote a real file into the source tree
  as a side effect, and a couple of resource/file-descriptor leaks.

